    description:
      `Capture live network traffic and dissect it packet-by-packet in Wireshark, labeling frames by protocol and OSI layer. This is the exact workflow used in real network traffic analysis and incident response to spot plaintext credential leaks, DNS tunneling, and C2 beaconing — reading a raw packet capture confidently is a hard requirement for any SOC or network security role.`,
    platform: "Wireshark",
    difficulty: "easy",
    estimatedHours: 2,
    instructions: [
      `Download and install Wireshark from wireshark.org/download.html, keeping the Npcap driver installation enabled`,
      `Start a live capture on your active Wi-Fi or Ethernet interface`,
      `Generate traffic: visit a non-HTTPS site (e.g. http://neverssl.com), run 'ping 8.8.8.8' in a terminal, and run 'nslookup example.com'`,
      `Stop the capture, then open Statistics > Protocol Hierarchy to see the percentage breakdown of every protocol you captured`,
      `Use the display filter bar to isolate and label one packet each for: arp, dns, tcp.flags.syn==1 (the 3-way handshake SYN/SYN-ACK/ACK), and http.request`,
      `Right-click the HTTP GET request packet -> Follow -> TCP Stream to view the full plaintext request and response`,
      `For extra practice, download one real pcap from Malware-Traffic-Analysis.net's training exercises page and use the same filters to find the suspicious traffic`,
      `Save your own capture as capture.pcapng and submit it alongside your labeled packet list`,
