      { title: "Windows Security Log Encyclopedia (ultimatewindowssecurity.com)", url: "https://www.ultimatewindowssecurity.com/securitylog/encyclopedia/", type: "link" },
      { title: "Get-WinEvent documentation (Microsoft Learn)", url: "https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/powershell/module/microsoft.powershell.diagnostics/get-winevent", type: "link" },
    ],
    completionIndicator:
      `Event Viewer screenshot with the 5 flagged suspicious Event IDs (e.g. 4625, 4720, 4732, 7045, 4688) visible together in the filtered results pane`,
  },
  {
    id: "L12",
    number: 12,
    week: 5,
    moduleId: "m4",
    title: "Inspect & manipulate session cookies with DevTools",
    description:
      `Use browser DevTools to inspect how a real login-based app manages session state in cookies vs. JavaScript-readable tokens, check the HttpOnly/Secure/SameSite flags that defend against theft, and tamper the cookie value to see how the server reacts. This is the foundational skill behind session hijacking, session fixation, and CSRF testing later in the programme — you can't attack (or defend) session handling you don't know how to read.`,
    platform: "Browser DevTools",
    difficulty: "easy",
    estimatedHours: 1,
    instructions: [
      `Run OWASP Juice Shop locally: docker run --rm -p 3000:3000 bkimminich/juice-shop, then browse to http://localhost:3000`,
      `Register an account and log in, then open DevTools (F12) before touching anything else`,
      `Go to Application (Chrome/Edge) or Storage (Firefox) -> Cookies -> http://localhost:3000 and find the cookie named 'token'`,
      `Record whether HttpOnly, Secure, and SameSite are set on it — note that Juice Shop's Angular frontend also needs to read the JWT client-side, so this cookie is a realistic case of a session token that is NOT fully locked down`,
      `In the DevTools Console, run document.cookie and check whether the token value is visible — this tells you whether HttpOnly is actually protecting it`,
      `Copy the JWT value and decode it with CyberChef's 'JWT Decode' operation (or jwt.io) to see the claims stored inside (email, isAdmin, etc.)`,
      `In Application -> Cookies, edit the cookie's value directly (change one character), reload the page, and observe how the app responds (logged out, error, or silently ignored)`,
      `Write 3-5 sentences: which flags were missing, why that matters for XSS/CSRF, and what you'd recommend a dev team change`,
    ],
    resources: [
      { title: "OWASP Juice Shop (GitHub)", url: "https://github.com/juice-shop/juice-shop", type: "tool" },
      { title: "bkimminich/juice-shop (Docker Hub)", url: "https://hub.docker.com/r/bkimminich/juice-shop", type: "tool" },
      { title: "MDN: Document.cookie", url: "https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/Document/cookie", type: "link" },
      { title: "MDN: Using HTTP cookies", url: "https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/HTTP/Guides/Cookies", type: "link" },
      { title: "OWASP Session Management Cheat Sheet", url: "https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Session_Management_Cheat_Sheet.html", type: "link" },
    ],
    completionIndicator:
      `browser screenshot showing DevTools Application/Storage panel with the Juice Shop token cookie's HttpOnly/Secure/SameSite flags visible, plus a Console screenshot of the document.cookie result and a short note on the effect of editing the cookie value`,
    dockerImage: "bkimminich/juice-shop",
    containerPort: 3000,
  },
  {
    id: "L13",
    number: 13,
    week: 5,
    moduleId: "m5",
    title: "Manually encode/decode strings in Base64 & URL encoding",
    description:
      `Encode and decode 5 strings by hand (or by reasoning through the algorithm) in both Base64 and percent/URL encoding, then verify every answer with the CyberChef 'Cyber Swiss Army Knife'. This drills the difference between encoding (reversible, not secret) and encryption/hashing — a distinction trainees misuse constantly when reading real traffic captures or malware config blobs later in the course.`,
    platform: "CyberChef",
    difficulty: "easy",
    estimatedHours: 1,
    instructions: [
      `Pick 5 short strings of varying length, including at least one with spaces and one with & or = characters`,
      `For each string, manually work out the Base64 encoding on paper/whiteboard: group into 6-bit chunks and map to the A-Za-z0-9+/ alphabet, padding with = as needed`,
      `Verify each with the terminal: echo -n 'string' | base64`,
      `Open CyberChef at https://gchq.github.io/CyberChef/, drag the 'To Base64' operation into the Recipe pane, paste each string into Input, and confirm the Output matches your manual/terminal result`,
      `Do the same for percent/URL encoding: manually convert each special character to %XX (its hex ASCII value), noting that a space becomes %20 (or + in form-encoded contexts)`,
      `Verify with CyberChef's 'URL Encode' operation, then chain 'URL Decode' immediately after it in the same recipe to confirm you get the original string back`,
      `Save one CyberChef recipe as a shareable URL (Recipe pane -> the link icon) and note how the recipe and input are embedded directly in the URL`,
      `Write one sentence per string explaining a real scenario where you'd need to reverse this encoding (e.g. a URL-encoded query string in a Burp request, a Base64 blob in an HTTP header)`,
    ],
    resources: [
      { title: "CyberChef", url: "https://gchq.github.io/CyberChef/", type: "tool" },
      { title: "CyberChef source (GitHub)", url: "https://github.com/gchq/CyberChef", type: "link" },
      { title: "MDN: Percent-encoding glossary", url: "https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Glossary/Percent-encoding", type: "link" },
      { title: "RFC 4648: The Base16, Base32, and Base64 Data Encodings", url: "https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc4648", type: "link" },
    ],
    completionIndicator:
      `screenshot showing all 5 strings' manual/terminal Base64 and URL-encoding results matching CyberChef's Recipe output pane exactly, with the decode step confirming round-trip correctness`,
  },
  {
    id: "L14",
    number: 14,
    week: 5,
    moduleId: "m5",
    title: "Generate RSA keypair with OpenSSL, sign & verify a file",
    description:
      `Generate a real 2048-bit RSA keypair with OpenSSL, use the private key to digitally sign a file, verify the signature with only the public key, then prove the signature breaks the moment the file changes. This is the exact mechanism behind code-signing, TLS certificates, and SSH key auth — understanding sign/verify with your own keys makes those systems far less of a black box.`,
    platform: "OpenSSL / Terminal",
    difficulty: "medium",
    estimatedHours: 2,
    instructions: [
      `Generate a 2048-bit RSA private key: openssl genrsa -out private.pem 2048 (or the modern equivalent: openssl genpkey -algorithm RSA -pkeyopt rsa_keygen_bits:2048 -out private.pem)`,
      `Derive the public key from it: openssl rsa -in private.pem -pubout -out public.pem`,
      `Inspect the key's modulus and exponent: openssl rsa -in private.pem -text -noout | head -20`,
      `Create a test file (echo 'integrity test' > file.txt) and sign its SHA-256 hash: openssl dgst -sha256 -sign private.pem -out file.sig file.txt`,
      `Inspect the signature bytes: xxd file.sig | head, noting it's a fixed-size binary blob regardless of the input file's length`,
      `Verify the signature using only the public key: openssl dgst -sha256 -verify public.pem -signature file.sig file.txt (expect 'Verified OK')`,
      `Modify file.txt slightly (echo 'tampered' >> file.txt) and re-run the verify command — confirm it now prints 'Verification Failure'`,
      `Try verifying file.sig against a *different* file's public key or a re-generated keypair's public key, and explain in one sentence why that also fails`,
    ],
    resources: [
      { title: "OpenSSL documentation", url: "https://www.openssl.org/docs/", type: "link" },
      { title: "openssl-genpkey manual", url: "https://docs.openssl.org/3.1/man1/openssl-genpkey/", type: "link" },
      { title: "openssl-dgst manual", url: "https://docs.openssl.org/3.1/man1/openssl-dgst/", type: "link" },
      { title: "OpenSSL Cookbook: Key Generation (Feisty Duck)", url: "https://www.feistyduck.com/library/openssl-cookbook/online/openssl-command-line/key-generation.html", type: "link" },
    ],
    completionIndicator:
      `terminal output showing 'Verified OK' on the untouched file, followed by 'Verification Failure' after the file was modified, with both the sign and verify commands visible in scrollback`,
  },
  {
    id: "L15",
    number: 15,
    week: 6,
    moduleId: "m6",
    title: "Design a segmented corporate network diagram in draw.io",
    description:
      `Design a segmented corporate network diagram incorporating a DMZ, internal VLANs, an Active Directory domain controller, and Zero Trust controls, labelling every security control used. This forces you to reason about traffic-flow rules between zones the way a network/security architect does — the same segmentation logic you'll later validate (or attack) with Nmap and BloodHound.`,
    platform: "draw.io",
    difficulty: "medium",
    estimatedHours: 3,
    instructions: [
      `Open https://app.diagrams.net (draw.io) and start a new blank diagram, or use the built-in 'Basic Network' template`,
      `In the left shape panel, click 'More Shapes' -> enable the 'Networking' (and optionally AWS/Azure or Cisco) stencil libraries -> Apply`,
      `Add an internet edge (cloud shape) -> perimeter firewall -> a DMZ segment hosting a public-facing web server`,
      `Add at least 3 internal VLANs (e.g. HR, IT, Finance) connected through a Layer-3 switch/router, with each VLAN's subnet CIDR labelled`,
      `Add an Active Directory domain controller inside the internal network, connected to the VLANs it authenticates`,
      `Annotate Zero Trust controls: IAM/MFA at the access edge, micro-segmentation boundaries between VLANs, and IDS/IPS/WAF placement at the DMZ and perimeter`,
      `Use arrows/labels to show which traffic flows are permitted between zones (e.g. 'DMZ -> Internal: denied except port 443 to app tier')`,
      `Export the diagram as PNG or PDF (File -> Export as) and write a short paragraph explaining the traffic-flow rules and why each control is placed where it is`,
    ],
    resources: [
      { title: "draw.io / diagrams.net", url: "https://app.diagrams.net", type: "tool" },
      { title: "draw.io: Drawing network and infrastructure diagrams", url: "https://www.drawio.com/docs/diagram-types/network-diagrams/", type: "link" },
      { title: "draw.io: Network and technical diagram shapes", url: "https://drawio-app.com/blog/network-and-technical-diagram-shapes-in-draw-io/", type: "link" },
      { title: "NIST SP 800-207: Zero Trust Architecture", url: "https://csrc.nist.gov/pubs/sp/800/207/final", type: "link" },
    ],
    completionIndicator:
      `exported PNG/PDF of the network diagram showing a labelled DMZ, at least 3 labelled VLANs with CIDRs, an Active Directory domain controller, IDS/IPS/WAF placement, and annotated Zero Trust controls with traffic-flow rules between zones`,
  },
  {
