  {
    id: "L20",
    number: 20,
    week: 8,
    moduleId: "m8",
    title: "Step-by-step Burp Suite setup",
    description:
      `Configure Burp Suite Community Edition as an intercepting proxy in front of Firefox using FoxyProxy, then install Burp's CA certificate so HTTPS traffic can be decrypted and inspected without browser warnings. This is the foundational skill every later lab in this course depends on (L21-L32) and the first thing any real web app pentest engagement requires: without a working intercept setup, Proxy history, Repeater, and Intruder are all unusable.`,
    platform: "Burp Suite",
    difficulty: "easy",
    estimatedHours: 1,
    instructions: [
      `Download and install Burp Suite Community Edition from portswigger.net/burp/communitydownload`,
      `Launch Burp, accept the license, choose 'Temporary project' and 'Use Burp defaults', then click Start Burp`,
      `In Burp go to Proxy > Proxy settings and confirm a proxy listener is running on 127.0.0.1:8080`,
      `In Firefox, install the 'FoxyProxy Standard' extension from addons.mozilla.org`,
      `In FoxyProxy's options, add a new proxy titled 'Burp' with IP 127.0.0.1 and port 8080, save it, then click the FoxyProxy toolbar icon and select it to route all Firefox traffic through Burp`,
      `With FoxyProxy enabled, browse to http://burp (or http://127.0.0.1:8080) and click 'CA Certificate' in the top-right to download cacert.der`,
      `In Firefox go to Settings > Privacy & Security > Certificates > View Certificates > Authorities tab > Import, select cacert.der, and check 'Trust this CA to identify websites'`,
      `In Burp, go to Proxy > Intercept and confirm 'Intercept is off' so traffic flows through passively for this test`,
      `Browse to any HTTPS site in the FoxyProxy-enabled Firefox and confirm the padlock shows no warning and the request appears in Proxy > HTTP history`,
      `Screenshot Burp's HTTP history showing at least one captured HTTPS request/response pair as evidence of a working intercept setup`,
    ],
    resources: [
      { title: "Burp Suite Community Edition download", url: "https://portswigger.net/burp/communitydownload", type: "tool" },
      { title: "PortSwigger: Installing Burp's CA certificate in Firefox", url: "https://portswigger.net/burp/documentation/desktop/external-browser-config/certificate/ca-cert-firefox", type: "link" },
      { title: "PortSwigger: Configuring Firefox to work with Burp", url: "https://portswigger.net/burp/documentation/desktop/external-browser-config/browser-config-firefox", type: "link" },
      { title: "FoxyProxy Standard (Firefox add-on)", url: "https://addons.mozilla.org/en-US/firefox/addon/foxyproxy-standard/", type: "tool" },
      { title: "PortSwigger Web Academy", url: "https://portswigger.net/web-security", type: "platform" },
    ],
    completionIndicator:
      `Screenshot of Burp Suite's Proxy > HTTP history tab showing a captured HTTPS request (status 200) to a real site, taken with FoxyProxy enabled in Firefox and no 'certificate not trusted' warning displayed.`,
  },
  {
    id: "L21",
    number: 21,
    week: 8,
    moduleId: "m8",
    title: "Burp Intercept: capture login request on DVWA",
    description:
      `Stand up DVWA locally in Docker, then use Burp's Intercept to catch the raw login POST request before it reaches the server and tamper with its parameters. This teaches the core proxy workflow every later attack (SQLi, XSS, IDOR) relies on: seeing and editing a request before it is sent, rather than trusting whatever the browser's form UI allows.`,
    platform: "Burp Suite / DVWA",
    difficulty: "easy",
    estimatedHours: 1,
    instructions: [
      `Pull and run the DVWA container: docker run -d --name dvwa -p 8080:80 ghcr.io/digininja/dvwa:latest`,
      `Browse to http://localhost:8080, click 'Create / Reset Database', and wait for setup to finish`,
      `Log in with the default credentials admin / password`,
      `Go to DVWA Security (left nav) and set Security Level to Low, then click Submit`,
      `With FoxyProxy enabled and Burp's Proxy > Intercept switched to 'Intercept is on', log out and resubmit the DVWA login form`,
      `Observe the captured raw POST request in Burp, including the username and password parameters and the PHPSESSID cookie`,
      `Right-click the intercepted request and choose 'Send to Repeater' to keep a copy for later, then modify the password parameter's value directly in the Intercept view`,
      `Click Forward to send the tampered request and observe DVWA's response (invalid login message)`,
      `Repeat once more with Intercept off to compare normal end-to-end traffic against the intercepted/tampered version`,
      `Screenshot the intercepted request showing your edited password parameter before you clicked Forward`,
    ],
    resources: [
      { title: "DVWA GitHub repository", url: "https://github.com/digininja/DVWA", type: "tool" },
      { title: "DVWA container image (GHCR)", url: "https://github.com/digininja/DVWA/pkgs/container/dvwa", type: "tool" },
      { title: "PortSwigger: Using Burp Proxy", url: "https://portswigger.net/burp/documentation/desktop/getting-started/proxy-intercept-traffic", type: "link" },
      { title: "OWASP Testing Guide", url: "https://owasp.org/www-project-web-security-testing-guide/", type: "link" },
    ],
    completionIndicator:
      `Screenshot of Burp's Proxy > Intercept panel showing the raw DVWA login POST request with the password parameter visibly edited to a different value, prior to clicking Forward.`,
    dockerImage: "ghcr.io/digininja/dvwa:latest",
    containerPort: 80,
  },
  {
    id: "L22",
    number: 22,
    week: 8,
    moduleId: "m8",
    title: "Burp Repeater: manual SQLi test on DVWA",
    description:
      `Use Burp Repeater to manually probe DVWA's SQL Injection page with hand-crafted payloads across all three configurable security levels, building the muscle memory for real-world SQLi discovery: form a hypothesis, send one modified request at a time, read the response diff, and adjust — instead of relying on an automated scanner.`,
    platform: "Burp Suite",
    difficulty: "medium",
    estimatedHours: 2,
    instructions: [
      `Run DVWA in Docker (docker run -d --name dvwa -p 8080:80 ghcr.io/digininja/dvwa:latest), create the database, log in as admin/password, and set Security Level to Low`,
