    moduleId: "m10",
    title: "PortSwigger: Business Logic Vulnerabilities - 2 labs",
    description:
      `Complete a price-manipulation lab and a workflow-validation-bypass lab on PortSwigger Web Academy's Business Logic Vulnerabilities path, learning to find flaws that no input filter or WAF will ever catch because the requests themselves are perfectly well-formed -- only the application's trust assumptions are broken.`,
    platform: "PortSwigger",
    difficulty: "medium",
    estimatedHours: 3,
    instructions: [
      `Open 'Excessive trust in client-side controls' and log in with wiener:peter; note you have $100 store credit but the target item (a leather jacket) costs $1337, so a normal purchase is rejected`,
      `Add the jacket to the cart and intercept the POST /cart request in Burp Repeater; observe it includes a price parameter supplied by the client rather than recalculated server-side`,
      `Change the price parameter to a value at or below your $100 balance, resend the request, then refresh the cart in the browser to confirm the tampered price stuck`,
      `Complete checkout at the tampered price and confirm the lab is marked solved, then note in your write-up that the server never re-derived the price from the product catalog`,
      `Open 'High-level logic vulnerability'; note the same jacket-purchase constraint, but this time the discount mechanism is a coupon code (e.g. one code from the page header, a second earned by signing up for the newsletter)`,
      `Apply the first coupon, then the second, then re-apply the first again -- confirm the discount stacks further each time rather than being blocked as already-used, because the server only checks the immediately-preceding state rather than tracking total redemptions`,
      `Repeat the toggle-and-reapply sequence until the discounted price is at or below your available credit, complete checkout, and confirm the lab is solved`,
      `For both labs, write one sentence identifying the missing server-side control (price re-validation vs. coupon redemption tracking) -- this is the actual finding, not just the successful purchase`,
    ],
    resources: [
      { title: "PortSwigger Web Security Academy: Business logic vulnerabilities (topic overview)", url: "https://portswigger.net/web-security/logic-flaws", type: "platform" },
      { title: "Lab: Excessive trust in client-side controls", url: "https://portswigger.net/web-security/logic-flaws/examples/lab-logic-flaws-excessive-trust-in-client-side-controls", type: "platform" },
      { title: "Lab: High-level logic vulnerability", url: "https://portswigger.net/web-security/logic-flaws/examples/lab-logic-flaws-high-level", type: "platform" },
      { title: "OWASP Web Security Testing Guide: Business Logic Testing", url: "https://owasp.org/www-project-web-security-testing-guide/", type: "link" },
    ],
    completionIndicator:
      `Both labs show PortSwigger's 'Lab solved' banner after completing checkout on the l33t leather jacket; screenshots should include the Burp Repeater request showing the tampered price parameter (lab 1) and the sequence of coupon-application requests showing the stacking discount (lab 2).`,
  },
